Revision history for HTTP::libsfparse

0.002   (unreleased)
        - Incompatible change. Renamed the module from HTTP::SFParse to
          HTTP::libsfparse, after the C library it binds. The distribution
          is now HTTP-libsfparse, lib/HTTP/libsfparse.pm and libsfparse.xs.
          Exception messages carry the new name. The parse_item, parse_list
          and parse_dictionary functions, the SFPARSE_TYPE_* and SFP_*
          constants and the :types and :index tags are all unchanged.
          Entries below that name HTTP::SFParse describe releases made
          under the old name.
        - Incompatible change. Every parsed item is now an array reference
          of three elements, [ type, value, params ], rather than a hash
          reference. All three are always present, so indexing into an item
          never has to check its type first. A decimal is reported as a
          single number: RFC 9651 allows at most twelve digits before the
          point and three after, so every legal decimal times a thousand is
          a whole number below 2**53 and nothing is lost by it.
        - Incompatible change. A value's type is now one of the constants
          SFPARSE_TYPE_BOOLEAN, SFPARSE_TYPE_INTEGER, SFPARSE_TYPE_DECIMAL,
          SFPARSE_TYPE_STRING, SFPARSE_TYPE_TOKEN, SFPARSE_TYPE_BYTESEQ,
          SFPARSE_TYPE_INNER_LIST, SFPARSE_TYPE_DATE and
          SFPARSE_TYPE_DISPSTRING, rather than a string such as 'integer'.
          These are libsfparse's own sfparse_type enumerators and carry its
          values, which are read from the C enum at compile time so that
          re-vendoring a library that renumbers it cannot leave Perl with
          stale constants. Import them individually or as ":types".
        - Incompatible change. parse_dictionary returns an array reference
          of [ key, item ] pairs in field order, and parameters take the
          same form, both keeping duplicate keys. The "ordered" option is
          gone, since that is now the only shape; the parsers take the field
          value and nothing else. Collapsing the pairs the way RFC 9651
          resolves a repeated key is one line:
            my %dict = map { $_->[SFP_KEY] => $_->[SFP_ITEM] } @$dict;
        - The positions within an item are available as the constants
          SFP_TYPE, SFP_VALUE and SFP_PARAMS, and the two halves of a
          [ key, item ] pair as SFP_KEY and SFP_ITEM, so that a subscript
          says what it reads.
          Import them individually or as ":index". They are defined
          alongside the C code that builds the structures, so they cannot
          fall out of step with the layout.
        - Incompatible change. Removed HTTP::SFParse::Parser. The interface
          is now the three functions and nothing else. Each of them takes
          its own copy of the field value, drives one parser from start to
          finish inside a single call, and frees both before returning, so
          there is no parser object to keep alive, no call order for a
          caller to get wrong, and no state shared between calls. What it
          offered that the functions cannot do is stopping early, which was
          measurably worth having only on field values far larger than HTTP
          sends. The pull parser is still there in C for anyone who wants to
          bind it again.
        - THREADS: with no parser objects there is nothing that must not be
          shared, so CLONE_SKIP is gone too. The functions are re-entrant
          and safe to call from several threads at once.
        - libsfparse is vendored unmodified again. Removing the pull parser
          removed the only way to reach the abort() calls its state machine
          makes when its entry points are used out of order, so the local
          patch that turned those into parse errors is gone, along with the
          test that kept it applied. Re-vendoring from upstream is now a
          straight copy of two files.

0.001   2026-11-19
        - First release.
        - XS binding to libsfparse (ngtcp2/sfparse), bundled at commit
          b9673f9c5d010955c9ce310856c22f8d1505383f.
        - parse_item, parse_list and parse_dictionary build Perl data
          structures in C, with an "ordered" option that keeps field order
          and duplicate keys.
        - HTTP::SFParse::Parser exposes the underlying pull parser.
        - Passes the httpwg structured-field-tests conformance suite, which
          is bundled in t/structured-field-tests/.
